WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.587
We can now combine the steps into one reusable function.

00:00:03.587 --> 00:00:09.712
The function receives a question, calls the dense retriever, builds the grounded prompt,

00:00:09.712 --> 00:00:15.667
sends that prompt to the language model, and returns both the answer and the sources.

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:19.507
This is our first complete RAG workflow.

00:00:19.507 --> 00:00:25.102
Notice that we also return the retrieved context, not only the final answer.

00:00:25.102 --> 00:00:27.496
That is an important design choice.

00:00:28.135 --> 00:00:33.547
When a user reports a bad answer, you want to inspect what the system retrieved.

00:00:33.547 --> 00:00:37.932
Without that information, debugging becomes guesswork.

00:00:37.932 --> 00:00:43.902
The function is still small because each responsibility is separated into its own function.

00:00:43.902 --> 00:00:48.063
This is also how I recommend structuring a larger project.

00:00:48.063 --> 00:00:56.118
Keep retrieval, prompt construction, generation, and evaluation separable so that you can improve one layer without

00:00:56.118 --> 00:00:58.031
rewriting everything else.
